1267:
(a) Any person who smokes in an area where smoking is prohibited by the provisions of this chapter shall be guilty of an ordinance violation, and shall, upon conviction thereof, be subject to the following penalties:
(1) A minimum fine of $100.00 for a first violation;
(2) A minimum fine of $200.00 for a second violation within one year;
(3) A minimum fine of $500.00 for each additional violation within one year.
(b) Any person, firm, corporation, business or other legal entity who owns, manages, operates, or otherwise controls a public place or place of employment and who fails to comply with the provisions of this chapter shall be guilty of an ordinance violation, and shall, upon conviction thereof, be subject to the following penalties:
(1) A minimum fine of $100.00 for a first violation;
(2) A minimum fine of $200.00 for a second violation within one year;
(3) A minimum fine of $500.00 for each additional violation within one year.
(c) In addition to the fines established by this section, violation of this chapter by a person who owns, manages, operates, or otherwise controls a public place or place of employment may result in the suspension or revocation of any permit or license issued for the premises at which the violation occurred.
(d) With respect to a violation of this chapter that is continuous in nature, each day the violation occurs shall constitute a separate offense for that business and each person violating the smoking prohibition may also be cited as a separate offense.
(Code 1990, § 9-11-15)

1273:
The following words, terms, or phrases, when used in this chapter, shall have the meaning ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
(a) Amusement game room means any location as provided in O.C.G.A. § 16-12-35(b), (c) or (d) where one or more bona fide coin operated amusement machines are operated that permit non-cash redemption as provided in O.C.G.A. § 16-12-35(d)(1)(B), (C), or a combination thereof.
(b) Bona fide coin operated amusement machine means the same as this term is defined in O.C.G.A. § 50-27-70(b)(2)(A) and (B) and any applicable regulations of the State of Georgia. Examples of bona fide coin operated amusement machines include, but are expressly not limited to, the following:
1. Pinball machines;
2. Console machines;
3. Video games;
4. Crane machines;
5. Claw machines;
6. Pusher machines;
7. Bowling machines;
8. Novelty arcade games;
9. Foosball or table soccer machines;
10. Miniature racetrack, football or golf machines;
11. Target or shooting gallery machines;
12. Basketball machines;
13. Shuffleboard machines;
14. Kiddie ride games;
15. Skee-Ball machines;
16. Air hockey machines;
17. Roll down machines;
18. Trivia machines;
19. Laser games;
20. Simulator games;
21. Virtual reality machines;
22. Maze games;
23. Racing games;
24. Coin operated pool table or coin operated billiard table as defined in O.C.G.A. § 43-8-1(3); and
25. Any other similar amusement machine which can be legally operated in Georgia.
The term "coin operated amusement machine" does not include the following:
1. Coin operated washing machines or dryers;
2. Vending machines which for payment of money dispense products or services;
3. Gas and electric meters;
4. Pay telephones;
5. Pay toilets;
6. Cigarette vending machines;
7. Coin operated scales;
8. Coin operated gumball machines;
9. Coin operated parking meters;
10. Coin operated television sets which provide cable or network programming;
11. Coin operated massage beds; and
12. Machines which are not legally permitted to be operated in Georgia.
(c) Class B machine shall have the same definition as found in O.C.G.A. § 50-27-70.
(d) Location means a business within the City that has complied with the provisions of the ordinances of the City relating to occupation taxes and business licenses and the entire office or area of the business in any one location owned or leased by the same proprietor or proprietors where the lessor or lessors allow the space to be used for business purposes.
(e) Location owner or location operator means an owner or operator of a business where one or more bona fide coin operated amusement machines are available for commercial use and play by the public, or it shall have the same definition as found in O.C.G.A. § 50-27-70, should that definition differ.
(f) Operator means any person, individual, firm company, association, corporation, or other business entity owning any bona fide coin operated amusement machine in this state, or shall have the same definition as found in O.C.G.A. § 50-27-70, should that definition differ.
(g) Owner means any person, individual, firm, company, association, corporation, or other business entity owning any bona fide coin operated amusement machine in this state, or shall have the same definition as O.C.G.A. § 50-27-70, should that definition differ.
(Ord. No. 1346, § 1, 11-15-2023)

1301:
(a) The license of a massage establishment may be revoked upon one or more of the following grounds:
(i) Failure of the holder to maintain initial requirements for obtaining the license;
(ii) The holder allows or permits any person who is not a licensed massage therapist to administer a massage in said establishment;
(iii) The premises in which the massage establishment is located are in violation of any federal, State, or County laws designated for the health, protection and safety of the occupants or general public;
(iv) The premises are in violation of the City's building or life safety codes;
(v) The original application or renewal thereof, contains materially false information; or the applicant has deliberately sought to falsify information contained therein;
(vi) The holder of the license, including any person with an ownership interest in the license, has been convicted, pled guilty, or entered a plea of nolo contendere to any felony, or to any misdemeanor involving moral turpitude, or has violated any laws relating to sodomy, aggravated sodomy, solicitation of sodomy, public indecency, prostitution, pimping, pandering, pandering by compulsion, masturbation for hire, distribution of obscene materials, distribution of material depicting nudity, or sexual conduct as defined under Georgia law;
(vii) Any of the license holder's employees, independent contractors or agents has been convicted, pled guilty, or entered a plea of nolo contendere to any felony, or to any misdemeanor involving moral turpitude, or has violated any laws relating to sodomy, aggravated sodomy, solicitation of sodomy, public indecency, prostitution, pimping, pandering, pandering by compulsion, masturbation for hire, distribution of obscene materials, distribution of material depicting nudity, or sexual conduct as defined under Georgia law, in connection with the operation of the massage establishment or on or about the premises of the massage establishment;
(viii) Failure of the holder to actively supervise and monitor the conduct of the employees, independent contractors, agents, customers, or others on the premises in order to protect the health, safety and welfare of the general public and the customers; or
(ix) The holder, his employees, agents, or independent contractors associated with the establishment have allowed to occur or have engaged in a violation of any part of this chapter.
(b) The license of a massage therapist may be revoked upon one or more of the following grounds:
(i) Failure of the holder to maintain initial requirements for obtaining the license;
(ii) The holder is guilty of fraud in the practice of massage, or fraud or deceit in his being licensed in the practice of massage;
(iii) The holder is engaged in the practice of massage under a false or assumed name, or is impersonating another therapist of a like or different name;
(iv) The holder is guilty of fraudulent, false, misleading or deceptive advertising or practices any other licensed profession without legal authority therefor;
(v) The holder has violated any of the provisions of this chapter;
(vi) The holder has violated any laws relating to sodomy, aggravated sodomy, solicitation of sodomy, public indecency, prostitution, pimping, pandering, pandering by compulsion, masturbation for hire, distribution of obscene materials, distribution of material depicting nudity, or sexual conduct as defined under Georgia law; or has been convicted, pled guilty, or entered a plea of nolo contendere to any felony, or to any misdemeanor involving moral turpitude;
(vii) The original application, or renewal thereof, contains materially false information; or the applicant has deliberately sought to falsify information contained therein; or
(viii) There has been the occurrence of a fact which would have barred the issuance of the original license.
(c) Any massage therapist or massage establishment who has his or her or its license or permit revoked shall be disqualified from reapplying for such a license or permit for a period of 12 months immediately following the date of revocation.
(Ord. No. 879, § 1, 6-20-2012)

1311:
(a) Each wrecker service must own the trucks and equipment they are using. No wrecker truck or equipment may be shared by any other wrecker service. Each wrecker must display a current license tag of the proper classification for each vehicle.
(b) Every wrecker shall have markings displaying the name of the wrecker service and its address and telephone number on each side of the wrecker. Said letters shall be a minimum of four-inch letters and a maximum of ten-inch letters in colors contrasting with the color of the wrecker. Each sign shall be professionally applied and shall be either painted or permanently affixed to the wrecker.
(c) Each wrecker may also bear an identifying design, monogram or insignia. Such design, monogram or insignia shall not be designed in such a way as to be misleading or deceptive to the public. Such identifying design, monogram or insignia shall be professionally applied and shall be either painted or permanently affixed.
(d) Each wrecker shall be equipped with the following minimum standard equipment at all times:
i. Two or more safety chains;
ii. A minimum of two class "C" fire extinguishers mounted in an accessible location;
iii. One wrecking or pry bar that is a minimum 30 inches length;
iv. One push type broom;
v. One "flathead" shovel or scoop;
vi. A sufficient amount of absorbent material to absorb fluid leakage from one vehicle;
vii. Dual rear adjustable flood lights with a minimum of 20,000 candlepower;
viii. A yellow or amber rotating or strobe light bar affixed to the top of the cab of the wrecker. These lights shall be visible from 360 degrees and for a distance of not less than 1,500 feet under normal conditions at night;
ix. One pair of magnetically mounted tow lights to be mounted at the rear of towed vehicles;
x. A minimum of three triangle reflectors or safety cones;
xi. A NHTSA approved reflective safety vest/shirt for each employee engaged in recovery operations;
xii. Two wheel chocks; and
xiii. If applicable, a device to prevent a towed vehicle from causing or being caused further damage, such as wheel dollies, etc.
(e) The wrecker service shall operate at least two wreckers, at least one of which shall be an 8,000-pound dual cable wrecker and at least one of which shall be a "rollback" wrecker. Each wrecker must be well maintained and in good condition at all times and comply with all applicable State, federal and local laws.
(f) Each wrecker service shall be subject to having its storage/impound facilities and its wreckers and other equipment inspected during normal business hours by a representative of the CPD or other authorized personnel.
(Ord. No. 1242, § 1, 2-17-2021)

1329:
A hookah bar or lounge shall:
(1) Prohibit gambling or other games of chance;
(2) During the time when the establishment is open for business, prohibit entry to and post signs at each entrance and exit clearly stating that a person under the age of 18 years of age if the hookah lounge is an accessory use to a nightclub and shall prohibit entry to a person under the age of 21 years of age if the hookah lounge is an accessory use to a drinking place;
(3) Prohibit the sale of malt beverages, wines, distilled spirits or other alcoholic beverages, unless and except a valid alcohol license has been issued under the provisions of Section 9-1-1 et seq. of this Code;
(4) Abide by the following air ventilation requirements: hookah bar or lounge must have and maintain a ventilation system that exhausts smoke from the business and is designed in accordance with the state building code standards for the occupancy classification in use. The air handling systems from the smoking area shall be independent from the main air handling system that serves all other areas of the building, and all air within the smoking area shall be exhausted directly to the outside by an exhaust fan. No air from the smoking area shall be recirculated to other parts of the building.
(5) During the hours of operation, the interior of the premises of a hookah bar or lounge shall be maintained with adequate illumination to make the conduct of patrons within the premise readily discernible to a person with normal vision.
(Ord. No. 1323, § 1, 5-17-2023)

1338:
A. Every owner, operator, keeper or proprietor of any hotel shall, without delay, report to the City Police Department any violations of law that were either witnessed or made known to them by a bona fide employee, patron, guest, visitor or other person on the premises.
B. Every owner, operator, keeper or proprietor of any hotel shall, at all times, maintain a manager on duty capable of assisting, communicating, and cooperating with the police or other law enforcement officials in maintaining the public health, welfare, and safety.
C. All bona fide employees of a hotel shall be required to wear clearly visible identification badges that identify said person as a bona fide employee of said hotel.
D. All information required to be procured and kept pursuant to this chapter shall be kept strictly confidential in accordance with state and federal law and shall not be provided to any person except to a federal or state law enforcement officer or to any officer empowered to enforce this chapter. Such information is subject to inspection at all times, provided that either the City obtains the consent of the owner, operator, keeper or proprietor of the hotel, or the City produces appropriate legal authorization through a warrant or a subpoena.
E. For the purposes of this chapter, the term "record" shall mean the hotel's electronic guest registration system that stores guest identifying information. If the hotel does not have an electronic guest registration system, then the hotel shall record the guest, patron and their guest's information in a paper record or reservation book. The following information, at a minimum, must be recorded at the time of registration and maintained for a period of no less than 180 days after the rental agreement's termination:
1. The full name, phone number, and home address of each patron and overnight guest. If the patron is a tourism company or other business, only the patron shall be required to provide this information;
2. The total number of occupants (patrons and guests) registered in each room;
3. The room number assigned to each patron and guest;
4. The day, month, year and time of arrival of each patron and guest;
5. The day, month, year each patron and each guest is scheduled to depart;
6. Upon departure, a record of the exact departure day, month, and year for each patron and guest;
7. The rate charged and amount collected for rental of the room;
8. The method of payment for each room;
9. Documentation used to verify a stay in excess of 180 consecutive days;
10. Documentation of housekeeping as required in Section 9-17-9; and
11. Documentation of the inspection of the hotel room to ensure compliance with the definition of good repair when said inspection is required by Section 9-17-4.
F. Every owner, operator, keeper or proprietor of any hotel shall require each patron to provide proper identification prior to renting a room when registering in person. Proper identification is defined as a current and valid government issued photo identification card such as a driver's license, military identification card, state identification card, or passport. A record of the provided identification shall be kept on file for the duration of the occupancy and for 180 additional days thereafter.
G. No owner, operator, keeper or proprietor of any hotel shall rent a room to any person under 18 years of age, unless such minor has been declared legally emancipated by a court of law. Prior to renting any room to any emancipated person under 18 years of age, the hotel shall require such minor to provide such hotel with a court order evidencing such minor's emancipation, and the court order shall be kept and maintained by the hotel for at least 180 days after the minor's dates of stay.
H. It shall be unlawful for any person to procure or provide lodging in any hotel, through misrepresentation or production of false identification, or identification which misrepresents the identity of the person procuring or sharing in such lodging.
I. A receipt showing payment received shall be provided to each guest.
(Ord. No. 1375, § 1, 6-26-2024)

1355:
(a) Except as may otherwise be provided in this chapter, upon approval by resolution of the City Council, the City Manager is authorized to designate and maintain by appropriate traffic control signs, markings and devices:
(1) Crosswalks at intersections where there is particular danger to pedestrians crossing the roadway;
(2) Other safety zones for pedestrians;
(3) Traffic lanes;
(4) Speed, parking, truck or other traffic control zones;
(5) Stop, yield, one-way and other directionals and devices; and
(6) Any other sign, marking, device or zone necessary for orderly and safe conditions on the streets of the City.
(b) All traffic control signs, signals, devices and markings shall conform to specifications in the Manual on Uniform Traffic Control Devices adopted by the State Transportation Board. All signs and signals required hereunder for a particular purpose shall so far as practicable be uniform as to type and location throughout the City. All traffic control devices so erected and not inconsistent with the provisions of State law or this Code shall be official traffic control devices of the City.
(c) Reserved.
(d) No person shall be charged with violating a traffic zone, marking, sign or other traffic control device unless appropriate signs, markings or devices are in fact operating or in existence on streets involved.
(e) Any violation of any traffic zone, marking, sign or other traffic control device established hereunder shall be a violation of this Code.
(Code 1978, § 9-1004; Code 1990, § 10-1-3; Ord. No. 890, § 1, 10-3-2012)
